body:
@jimmyfrasche Am I missing something or are you literally just proposing 2b from my OP plus using a special starting symbol for disambiguation? One issue with that is that ids today can have `$` or whatever else, so a disambiguation based on syntax would need to be a lot more elaborate to avoid clashes.

----

As much as I like the power of XPath, which still leaves CSS selectors in the dust (oh how I long for `descendant-or-self` in CSS! But I digress), using XPath here is not a great idea. Authors already struggle with nontrivial CSS selectors (from what I've seen many even avoid simple combinators and add classes instead — and no, I’m not talking about those doing it intentionally as part of BEM), expecting them to learn a whole different querying language, with arguably not amazing ergonomics is …kind of a big ask. I would much rather use CSS selectors, and if there are any gaps that make them unable to express some use cases, just fix the gaps which would *also* improve CSS, qSA, and many other selector-based DOM methods.

@tabatkins 
> Similar but quite distinct from this topic, we need a way to pierce shadows up and down for these references.

Thank you for emphasizing this. It's critical that the solution here either directly supports or has a plan (perhaps via :part or via delegation similar to aria) to support Shadow DOM.


@LeaVerou 
`<< .media-object >> .content > *` is the "lower bound" here `.content` or `.content > *`? This question makes me lean towards a functional syntax with `(...)`.

@tabatkins 
> I think the model I'm thinking of is: starting from the element, search up for the nearest match for the upper bound scope (using the root if the upper scope is omitted or doesn't match), then search down from that scope for the lower bound scope (with no lower bound if it's omitted or doesn't match), then find the first element in DOM order between those bounds that matches the selector.

This is what I referred to when I spoke of everyone reinventing lexical scoping. I can't think of a situation where you would want to do anything other than that (that I would recommend doing on a serious project, at any rate). I'd much rather just have a global attribute to delimit the scope boundaries and let name look up happen in those (and searching up the tree until there's a match). Less powerful but easier to understand and use. Block based lexical scoping won in programming languages because everything else caused too many problems.

@noamr The idea of a syntactic switch (as I understand it) is to restrict selector syntax so that it starts with that character _anyway_, not to introduce a separate character that needs to be trimmed off so that the value can be processed as a selector. I think it's worth it to try to make sure that the attribute value is a valid selector that can be fed directly into other APIs without extra parsing/processing.

For example, if the syntactic switch is `:`, then `:is(.foo)` or `:root .foo` would be valid, but `.foo` would still be processed as an id. If *all* non-alphanumeric selector characters were syntactic switches (`.+~>#:[&`) then the only selectors that would need rewriting are those that start with type selectors, which are unlikely to be common (without a scoping mechanism). We did something similar with the earlier CSS nesting syntax, before we figured out a way to make the general case work.

If it turns out that we cannot use a wide enough set of characters so that this is easy to explain to authors, my preference would be for either a new set of attributes with a consistent naming scheme, or some kind of separate opt-in that changes how the existing attributes behave.

A likely terrible idea, just for the sake of brainstorming: Treat as id by default, fall back to a selector if id is not found. Authors can then make sure to write their selectors in a way that minimizes conflicts. We could probably add an HTTPArchive JS query for the upcoming crawl (this is not easily doable via SQL) to see what % of websites have id references that are currently not found, but would match something if they were treated as selectors. Again, I'm not seriously suggesting this, just trying to think outside the box as it might inspire some more viable ideas.

@alice https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11506 though there isn't much in there… It was only introduced briefly, mainly to get it on people's radars.

@keithamus but I feel like that's exactly what's being tackled in here: *ids matter*, they do on the URL, they do on the global context pollution, they do for assistive technologies ... the current state is that *ids don't matter anymore* because to automate accessibility IDs are trashed all over the place without any meaning whatsoever, just to satisfy `labelled-by` requirements, right?

So I fully agree with you there that *IDs* are relevant, but latest 20 years of Web growth rather showed how these are either a limitation, when meanings matters too, or an abused legacy feature automated to satisfy requirements, which is (imho) a bigger bummer to deal with.

**edit** on that note, a URL that points at a label without any context attached doesn't look like the best the Web can offer neither, but that ship has sealed long time ago
